(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-7-cms-exam-7-master-exam-form-c"]={"title":"CMS I Exam 7 Master Exam — Form C","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 7","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 58-year-old woman has 8 months of progressive exertional dyspnea and a persistent nonproductive cough, with bibasilar dry rales and small lung volumes on examination. Which combination of total lung capacity and ratio of forced expiratory volume in 1 second to forced vital capacity is expected?","choices":["Normal capacity, normal ratio","High capacity, low ratio","Low capacity, normal or high ratio","Normal capacity, low ratio"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Interstitial lung disease stiffens the lung so it no longer expands, giving a restrictive pattern: total lung capacity and forced vital capacity fall while the ratio of forced expiratory volume in 1 second to forced vital capacity stays normal or rises. Her small lung volumes on examination fit. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 4, 23, 24, 26, 29"},{"q":"A 25-year-old woman has an overnight sleep recording. Compared with her first sleep cycle, what does her last cycle before morning show?","choices":["Deeper non-rapid eye movement sleep","No rapid eye movement period","A cycle under 30 minutes long","A longer rapid eye movement period"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Across a typical night, rapid eye movement periods grow longer in the later cycles, while the deepest non-rapid eye movement sleep falls in the first cycles. This fits rapid eye movement episodes lengthening as the sleeper becomes more rested. — CMS 1 Phases of Sleep - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 7, 9, 10, 6"},{"q":"Which statement about dyspnea in proven pulmonary embolism is accurate?","choices":["About 25% lack it","Nearly every patient has it","It marks a massive clot","It develops over several days"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Approximately 25% of patients with proven pulmonary embolism completely lack dyspnea, which is why shortness of breath cannot be relied on. When it does present as isolated dyspnea, the onset is sudden, over minutes to hours, at rest or solely with exertion. — Pulmonary Vascular Disorders 2026 - Gray.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"A 47-year-old man is admitted after a crush injury with prolonged poor tissue perfusion. Arterial blood gas: pH 7.31 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 29 mm Hg (normal 35 to 45 mm Hg), bicarbonate 14 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L). Which change should be expected from his acid-base disturbance itself?","choices":["Potassium moves into cells, lowering serum potassium","Hemoglobin holds oxygen more tightly, cutting delivery","Hydrogen ions move out of cells into the blood","Potassium moves out of cells, raising serum potassium"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A pH of 7.31 with a bicarbonate of 14 is a metabolic acidosis (poor perfusion and trauma cause lactic acidosis). In acidosis, hydrogen ions shift into cells and potassium shifts out of cells into the extracellular fluid, resulting in hyperkalemia. — PAJ5510 Acid Base Disorders II MBF2026sc.pptx, Slides 5, 45"},{"q":"A 30-year-old man has started work in building demolition, where he handles old asbestos insulation. He asks how long after exposure asbestos lung disease usually leads people to seek care. Which answer is accurate?","choices":["About 1 year after exposure","About 5 years after exposure","About 15 years after exposure","About 40 years after exposure"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Asbestosis develops over 10-20 years, and patients usually seek medical attention about 15 years after exposure. That long delay is why an exposure history matters in an older patient with dyspnea, and why a young worker's risk starts with exposure he has now. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 55, 66"},{"q":"A 44-year-old man has had nasogastric suction for 4 days after bowel surgery. His arterial pH is 7.49 (normal 7.35 to 7.45) and his bicarbonate 33 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L). For metabolic alkalosis, carbon dioxide is expected to rise 0.75 mm Hg per 1 mEq/L rise in bicarbonate, from baselines of 40 mm Hg and 25 mEq/L. What carbon dioxide is expected from appropriate compensation?","choices":["34 mm Hg","40 mm Hg","46 mm Hg","48 mm Hg"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Bicarbonate has risen 33 - 25 = 8 mEq/L. At 0.75 mm Hg per 1 mEq/L, carbon dioxide should rise 8 x 0.75 = 6, so the expected value is 40 + 6 = 46 mm Hg. In metabolic alkalosis the lungs compensate by retaining carbon dioxide. — PAJ5510 Acid Base Disorders II MBF2026sc.pptx, Slides 24, 53"},{"q":"Which disease is listed among the connective tissue causes of interstitial lung disease?","choices":["Systemic sclerosis","Silicosis","Sarcoidosis","Hypersensitivity pneumonitis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The connective tissue diseases that cause interstitial lung disease, with an autoimmune inflammatory and fibrotic response, include systemic lupus erythematosus, rheumatoid arthritis, ankylosing spondylitis, systemic sclerosis, Sjögren's syndrome and polymyositis-dermatomyositis. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 12, 18"},{"q":"A 41-year-old cheese maker has recurrent fever, chills, cough and dyspnea hours after handling moldy cheese at work. Hypersensitivity pneumonitis is diagnosed, and her symptoms are mild. What is the first step in treatment?","choices":["Identify and avoid the agent","Oral corticosteroids","Lung transplant evaluation","Inhaled bronchodilators alone"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Treatment of hypersensitivity pneumonitis starts by identifying the agent responsible and avoiding it. Prompt diagnosis is key to removing the patient from the irritating environment: symptoms can be reversible if caught early, while continued exposure may worsen the disease. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 65, 71"},{"q":"When counseling patients after a spontaneous pneumothorax, which group needs early surgical evaluation for safety?","choices":["Long-distance runners","Former smokers","Office workers","Pilots and divers"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Pilots and divers need early surgical options for safety: high-pressure activities such as diving carry severe recurrence dangers, and scuba diving is to be avoided. Video-assisted thoracoscopic surgery closes leaks to prevent future collapses. — Pleural Disorders 2026.pptx, Slides 21, 59, 60"},{"q":"A 67-year-old man has progressive dyspnea and a dry cough. Over the lower right chest there is dullness to percussion, decreased tactile fremitus (vibrations from the larynx poorly felt on the chest wall) and diminished breath sounds, with bronchial breath sounds at the upper edge of the dull area. Which condition best explains these findings?","choices":["Pleurisy","Pneumonia infiltrate","Pleural effusion","Pneumothorax"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Dyspnea and dry cough are the most common symptoms of pleural effusion. Fluid is dull to percussion and obstructs the transmission of vibrations, so fremitus decreases; breath sounds are diminished over the effusion, while bronchial breath sounds increase toward its top, exactly his examination. — Pleural Disorders 2026.pptx, Slides 12, 23, 85, 87, 88, 89"},{"q":"Neuroendocrine paraneoplastic symptoms arise when a lung carcinoma develops from cells that secrete polypeptide hormones; which type does this?","choices":["Adenocarcinoma","Small cell carcinoma","Squamous cell carcinoma","Hamartoma"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Small cell carcinoma can develop neuroendocrine paraneoplastic symptoms when it arises from cells that secrete polypeptide hormones, the neuroendocrine cells lining the bronchioles. Paraneoplastic syndromes are typically a consequence of the small cell type, such as excess antidiuretic hormone with hyponatremia. — Pulmonary Nodules and Neoplasms 2026.pptx, Slides 4, 6, 12, 16, 31, 32, 41, 46, 48"},{"q":"A 58-year-old man who quit smoking 30 years ago has an 8-mm nodule on computed tomography done for a rib fracture. It is a smoothly marginated solid nodule with dense calcification in its center and no fat. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Healed granuloma","Linear scar","Hamartoma","Squamous cell carcinoma"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A smoothly marginated solid nodule with central calcification is typical of a healed granuloma, an inflammatory nodule and one of the main examples of benign nodules. Central calcification is a benign pattern, whereas eccentric or stippled ground-glass calcification is a malignant pattern. — Pulmonary Nodules and Neoplasms 2026.pptx, Slides 12, 16, 21"},{"q":"A 70-year-old woman with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease and cor pulmonale has an arterial oxygen tension of 52 mmHg (normal 80-100) on room air. Her cough and wheeze are at baseline, and her wedge pressure is normal. Which therapy targets the main driver of her pulmonary hypertension?","choices":["Oral calcium channel blocker","Aggressive loop diuresis","Continuous supplemental oxygen","Oral endothelin antagonist"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Alveolar hypoxia is the single most important driver of pulmonary hypertension in chronic obstructive pulmonary disease: global hypoxia causes diffuse pulmonary vasoconstriction and a relentless rise in right ventricular afterload. For Group 3 disease, continuous supplemental oxygen is paramount because it reverses that hypoxic vasoconstriction. — Pulmonary Vascular Disorders 2026 - Gray.pptx, Slides 6, 15, 16, 28"},{"q":"In which state does a mixed, low-voltage electroencephalogram with sawtooth waves appear?","choices":["Stage N3 deep sleep","Rapid eye movement sleep","Stage N1 light sleep","Stage N2 sleep"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Rapid eye movement sleep shows a mixed, low-voltage electroencephalogram with sawtooth waves and beta activity similar to wakefulness, alongside irregular, sharply peaked eye movements and atonia of voluntary muscles. — CMS 1 Phases of Sleep - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 26, 27, 28"},{"q":"A 60-year-old man with a 40 pack-year history has a concerning 2-cm peripheral nodule on computed tomography. He is afraid of needles and asks whether a sputum test could rule out cancer instead of a biopsy. Which response is accurate?","choices":["A negative result rules it out","It can stage the cancer","It works best for peripheral tumors","It is not a sensitive test"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Sputum cytology is listed in the workup but is not sensitive, so a negative result cannot rule out cancer. It is mainly useful for squamous cell carcinoma, a central bronchial tumor that can be caught with sputum testing, while peripheral tumors such as adenocarcinoma and large cell carcinoma are not. Biopsy classes the tumor type. — Pulmonary Nodules and Neoplasms 2026.pptx, Slides 14, 22, 30, 40"},{"q":"What does bronchoalveolar lavage show in silicosis?","choices":["Thick, milky effluent","Noncaseating granulomas","Bloody hemorrhagic fluid","Ferruginous bodies"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. In silicosis, bronchoalveolar lavage returns a thick and milky effluent containing foamy, lipoproteinaceous macrophages. Other laboratory tests add little beyond an arterial blood gas, and pulmonary function shows reduced forced vital capacity and forced expiratory volume in 1 second. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 19, 44, 55, 62, 70"},{"q":"A 66-year-old man with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease has a symptomatic right pneumothorax on his upright chest radiograph. He is otherwise stable. Which intervention is contraindicated for him?","choices":["Thoracoscopic surgery","Catheter aspiration","Hospital admission","Chest tube insertion"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Underlying pulmonary disease contraindicates catheter aspiration, along with traumatic or tension pneumothorax, hemodynamic instability, recurrence, bilateral pneumothoraces and bleeding disorders. His chronic obstructive pulmonary disease makes this a secondary spontaneous pneumothorax, and most of those get a chest tube instead. — Pleural Disorders 2026.pptx, Slides 39, 49, 56, 57"},{"q":"A 30-year-old woman sits quietly with her eyes closed, relaxed but awake, during an electroencephalogram. The tracing shows rhythmical waves at 10 cycles per second, strongest over the occipital region. Which waveform is this?","choices":["Alpha waves","Delta waves","Theta waves","Beta waves"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Rhythmical waves at 8 to 13 cycles per second, most intense over the occipital region, are alpha waves. They are found in almost all healthy adults who are awake in a quiet, resting state, such as sitting relaxed with the eyes closed, and they disappear during deep sleep. — CMS 1 Phases of Sleep - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 22, 23, 25, 26, 28"},{"q":"A 69-year-old woman with heart failure has acute dyspnea. Her chest film shows central perihilar opacities, thickened, blurred bronchiole walls and short horizontal lines at the lung periphery that reach the pleura. What are the short horizontal peripheral lines called?","choices":["Hampton's hump","Peribronchial cuffing","Kerley B lines","Westermark sign"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Kerley B lines are short horizontal lines at the lung periphery, made by fluid engorging the interlobular septa. With central perihilar (batwing) opacities and peribronchial cuffing, they complete the chest film of cardiogenic pulmonary edema. — Pulmonary Vascular Disorders 2026 - Gray.pptx, Slides 37, 51"},{"q":"A 52-year-old man has spent 20 years cutting and polishing stone in a quarry. He now has progressive exertional dyspnea and a restrictive pattern on pulmonary function testing. How is his occupational exposure classified?","choices":["Fumes and gases","Treatment related","Organic dust","Inorganic dust"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Occupational and environmental exposures are grouped as inorganic dusts, organic dusts and fumes or gases. Stone work releases free crystalline silica, and pneumoconiosis is caused by inhaled coal dust or various inorganic or silicate dusts, so his exposure is an inorganic dust. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 12, 13, 50, 60, 65"},{"q":"A 78-year-old man with moderate dementia and limited-stage small cell lung carcinoma had a partial response to initial chemotherapy and radiation. He now needs help with most daily activities. His family asks about radiating his brain to prevent metastases. Which therapy should be avoided?","choices":["Brain magnetic resonance imaging","Prophylactic cranial irradiation","Chest and abdomen computed tomography","Smoking cessation support"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Prophylactic cranial irradiation is the usual adjuvant step for limited-stage disease after a complete or partial response, but it is not recommended in patients with poor performance status or impaired neurocognitive function. His dementia and dependence in daily activities are both reasons to avoid it. — Pulmonary Nodules and Neoplasms 2026.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"A 69-year-old man with a 40 pack-year smoking history has 18 months of insidious dry cough, exertional dyspnea and fatigue. He has clubbing, bibasilar inspiratory crackles, tachypnea and reduced chest expansion. Computed tomography shows diffuse, patchy fibrosis with pleural-based honeycombing. He has no occupational exposures, medicines or connective tissue disease. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Asbestosis","Sarcoidosis","Hypersensitivity pneumonitis","Idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Insidious dry cough, exertional dyspnea, fatigue, clubbing, bibasilar inspiratory crackles, tachypnea and reduced chest expansion are the picture of idiopathic fibrosing interstitial pneumonia, and pleural-based honeycombing with no associated cause is idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis. Most patients have a smoking history. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 31, 33, 34, 35, 40, 55, 58, 65"},{"q":"A 50-year-old man has a polysomnogram as part of a research study. He has no sleepiness, fatigue or insomnia, and no one has noted snoring or pauses in his breathing. The study shows 7 predominantly obstructive events per hour. How should this result be interpreted?","choices":["Criteria for diagnosis are not met","Needs positive airway pressure therapy","Diagnostic of central sleep apnea","Diagnostic of obstructive sleep apnea"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Without symptoms, the diagnosis needs 15 or more predominantly obstructive events per hour; 5 or more counts only with a symptom such as sleepiness, fatigue, insomnia, choking or snoring noted by an observer. He has 7 events and no symptoms, so neither criterion is met. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 9, 12, 26"},{"q":"A 66-year-old man with lactic acidosis from postoperative sepsis receives an opioid by a pump and is now drowsy with shallow breathing. Arterial blood gas: pH 7.15 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 36 mm Hg (normal 35 to 45 mm Hg), bicarbonate 12 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L); sodium 137 mEq/L (normal 135 to 145 mEq/L), chloride 101 mEq/L (normal 98 to 106 mEq/L). Which part of his history best explains his concurrent respiratory acidosis?","choices":["Anxiety after surgery","The opioid pain medicine","Saline given in surgery","The postoperative sepsis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Expected carbon dioxide = 1.5 x 12 + 8 = 26, range 24 to 28; his 36 is above it, a concurrent respiratory acidosis. Respiratory depressants such as narcotics cause decreased ventilation, matching his drowsiness and shallow breathing. — PAJ5510 Acid Base Disorders II MBF2026sc.pptx, Slides 49, 32, 36"},{"q":"A 58-year-old man with a body mass index of 37 kg/m2 has an awake arterial carbon dioxide pressure of 51 mm Hg (normal 35-45). He has not yet had a chest radiograph, pulmonary function tests, thyroid function tests or a toxicology screen. What must happen before obesity hypoventilation syndrome can be diagnosed?","choices":["Start bilevel pressure therapy first","Document coexisting obstructive apnea","Confirm a body mass index above 35","Exclude other causes of hypercapnia"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Obesity hypoventilation syndrome is a diagnosis of exclusion: obesity (body mass index above 30) and awake hypercapnia (arterial carbon dioxide pressure above 45 mm Hg) must be joined by exclusion of alternative causes of hypercapnia and hypoventilation. The workup includes a chest radiograph, pulmonary function tests, thyroid function tests and a toxicology screen. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 32, 30, 31, 33, 17"},{"q":"A 74-year-old woman says she no longer sleeps as deeply as she did in her twenties. Which sleep stage can she be told decreases with age?","choices":["Stage N2","Stage N1","Rapid eye movement sleep","Stage N3"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Stage N3, the deep, restful, deepest non-rapid eye movement stage with delta waves, decreases with age. Her sense that her sleep is less deep than it was in her twenties fits a smaller share of stage N3 sleep. — CMS 1 Phases of Sleep - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 28, 6"},{"q":"A 68-year-old man who still smokes and has a 70 pack-year history has 2 months of cough, wheezing and hemoptysis. Computed tomography shows a large, cavitated central mass arising from a bronchus. A metabolic panel shows a serum calcium of 12.8 mg/dL (normal 8.5-10.5). Which lung cancer type is most likely?","choices":["Squamous cell carcinoma","Adenocarcinoma","Large cell carcinoma","Small cell carcinoma"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Squamous cell carcinoma has the strongest association with smoking, sits centrally and often originates in a bronchus, cavitates frequently, and causes cough, wheezing and hemoptysis once it obstructs the airway. Hypercalcemia is a paraneoplastic finding seen particularly with squamous cell carcinoma, and his calcium of 12.8 exceeds the 10.5 ceiling. — Pulmonary Nodules and Neoplasms 2026.pptx, Slides 14, 30, 31, 33, 39, 41, 46, 48"},{"q":"A 64-year-old woman is 5 days out from recent spine surgery when she collapses with a massive pulmonary embolism. Her blood pressure has been 72/44 mmHg for 30 minutes. Which intervention fits?","choices":["Mechanical thrombectomy or embolectomy","Catheter-directed thrombolysis","Full-dose systemic thrombolysis","Anticoagulation alone, then reassess"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Thrombolysis is the standard for massive, hemodynamically unstable embolism, but recent surgery is an absolute contraindication to it. Mechanical thrombectomy or surgical embolectomy, which physically extracts or macerates the clot, is reserved for exactly this patient: massive embolism with a contraindication to thrombolytics. — Pulmonary Vascular Disorders 2026 - Gray.pptx, Slides 58, 60"},{"q":"A 49-year-old woman with idiopathic pulmonary arterial hypertension faints while climbing two flights of stairs. Echocardiography shows a dilated right ventricle, a septum bowing into the left ventricle and normal left ventricular contractility. What explains her exertional syncope?","choices":["Impaired left ventricular filling","A drop in pulmonary resistance","Weak left ventricular contraction","Right ventricular hypertrophy alone"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. In advanced pulmonary hypertension the dilated right ventricle pushes the septum into the left ventricle, so the left ventricle fills poorly in diastole and output falls, even though its contractility is normal. Syncope on exertion is ominous: it signals a fixed low output that cannot rise with effort. — Pulmonary Vascular Disorders 2026 - Gray.pptx, Slides 20, 21"},{"q":"A 23-year-old man has a first primary spontaneous pneumothorax with an apex-to-cupola distance of 2 cm on his upright chest radiograph. He has mild pleuritic pain, no significant dyspnea and stable vital signs. Which is the most appropriate management?","choices":["Thoracoscopic pleurodesis","Catheter aspiration","Chest tube insertion","Observation with 100% oxygen"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A small primary spontaneous pneumothorax, under 3 cm at the apex, with minimal symptoms is observed with adequate analgesia and oxygen at 100% by non-rebreather mask, which accelerates resorption of air. A repeat film confirms it has not expanded, and he is discharged if it is stable and he is asymptomatic. — Pleural Disorders 2026.pptx, Slides 53, 54, 55"},{"q":"A patient has a serum albumin of 2.5 g/dL (normal 3.5 to 5.5 g/dL) and a measured anion gap of 10 (normal 6 to 12). What is the corrected anion gap?","choices":["10","12.5","15","17.5"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The anion gap is corrected by adding 2.5 for each 1 g/dL that serum albumin falls below 4.5 g/dL. Albumin 2.5 is 2 g/dL below 4.5, so add 2.5 x 2 = 5: corrected gap 10 + 5 = 15, which is raised although the measured gap looked normal. — PAJ5510 Acid Base Disorders II MBF2026sc.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"A 38-year-old woman has been bedbound at home for 7 days with severe influenza and now has sudden dyspnea. The clinician judges pulmonary embolism more likely than any alternative diagnosis, giving a Wells score of 4.5. Her heart rate is 92/min and oxygen saturation 97%, and she has no hemoptysis, no estrogen use, no prior clots, no surgery or trauma and no leg swelling, so she would meet every PERC (Pulmonary Embolism Rule-out Criteria) item. Her kidney function is normal and she has no contrast allergy. Which step fits?","choices":["Computed tomographic pulmonary angiography","Stop: she meets every rule-out item","Ventilation-perfusion scan first","A D-dimer, then imaging if positive"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The rule-out criteria come after the Wells score and are used when it is 4 or less. Her score of 4.5 (embolism more likely than alternatives 3 + immobilization for more than 3 days 1.5) makes embolism likely, so the D-dimer and the rule-out criteria are bypassed and she goes directly to computed tomographic pulmonary angiography. — Pulmonary Vascular Disorders 2026 - Gray.pptx, Slides 53, 54, 57"},{"q":"A 36-year-old woman with a hemothorax after a stab wound has a chest tube placed. Over the next 2 hours it drains blood at about 300 mL per hour, and her blood pressure is falling. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Thoracotomy","Continued tube drainage","Needle decompression","Chemical pleurodesis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The chest tube evacuates and quantifies the bleeding, and blood loss over 200 mL per hour calls for thoracotomy, an open approach to the intrathoracic space. Persistent hemorrhage or shock is the indication for surgery, and she has both. — Pleural Disorders 2026.pptx, Slides 45, 66, 69, 74"},{"q":"A 64-year-old man with a 50 pack-year history is diagnosed with small cell lung carcinoma after presenting with bone pain. Computed tomography shows a mass beside the left hilum. His wife asks where this cancer usually arises and why it was not caught earlier. Which response is accurate?","choices":["Central; rarely spreads early","Peripheral; caught by sputum","Peripheral; easy to find early","Central; often spread at diagnosis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Small cell carcinoma has a central location and infiltrates the mucosa, so it develops insidiously, and over 70% of patients are metastatic, to the liver, bone or brain, before detection. His hilar mass and bone pain fit that pattern: central, and already spread when found. — Pulmonary Nodules and Neoplasms 2026.pptx, Slides 30, 40, 41"},{"q":"A 34-year-old new mother has been sleeping about 4 hours a night for a week and has gone back to her office job. Which change is expected from this sleep restriction?","choices":["Better physical performance","More output from longer waking days","Reduced productivity at work","No change for at least a month"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Sleep restriction over a few days can decrease cognitive and physical performance, overall productivity and the health of a person. A week of about 4 hours a night is restriction over more than a few days, so lower productivity at work is expected. — CMS 1 Phases of Sleep - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"A 45-year-old woman with idiopathic pulmonary arterial hypertension remains in functional class IV despite maximal medical therapy, including a continuous intravenous prostanoid. Which referral offers the only definitive option?","choices":["Left ventricular assist device","Pulmonary thromboendarterectomy","Mitral valve replacement","Lung transplantation evaluation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. For pulmonary arterial hypertension refractory to maximal medical therapy (or pulmonary veno-occlusive disease), lung transplantation is the only definitive option, with continuous intravenous support as a bridge. Early palliative care discussions belong alongside the referral. — Pulmonary Vascular Disorders 2026 - Gray.pptx, Slides 19, 29, 44"},{"q":"A 58-year-old man with pulmonary arterial hypertension has no signs of right ventricular failure. His symptoms have progressed moderately over the past year, he has fainted once, he is in World Health Organization functional class III, and his six-minute walk distance is 310 m. Which prognostic risk band fits him?","choices":["Low risk, under 5% at 1 year","Intermediate risk, 5-10% at 1 year","Intermediate risk, over 10% at 1 year","High risk, over 10% at 1 year"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Each feature sits in the middle row: no right ventricular failure signs, moderate progression, occasional syncope, functional class III and a six-minute walk distance between 165 and 440 m. Intermediate risk carries an estimated 1-year mortality of 5-10%. — Pulmonary Vascular Disorders 2026 - Gray.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"A 71-year-old woman has a new left pleural effusion with no clear clinical cause, and diagnostic thoracentesis is performed. Pleural fluid protein is 2.1 g/dL with serum protein 7.0 g/dL (normal 6.0-8.3 g/dL). Pleural fluid lactate dehydrogenase is 210 units/L with serum lactate dehydrogenase 450 units/L (normal 140-280 units/L). How is the effusion classified by Light's criteria?","choices":["Exudate, by lactate dehydrogenase ratio","Exudate, by lactate dehydrogenase level","Exudate, by protein ratio","Transudate, no criterion met"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Protein ratio 2.1 / 7.0 = 0.30, not above 0.5. Lactate dehydrogenase ratio 210 / 450 = 0.47, not above 0.6. But fluid lactate dehydrogenase 210 exceeds two-thirds of the 280 upper limit of normal serum lactate dehydrogenase, about 187: met, so the fluid is an exudate. — Pleural Disorders 2026.pptx, Slides 96, 101, 110, 112"},{"q":"A 23-year-old woman has sudden fever and paroxysms of knife-like pleuritic pain lasting 15-30 minutes after a gastrointestinal illness, with tender intercostal muscles and a normal chest radiograph. Which laboratory finding supports the suspected diagnosis?","choices":["Purulent pleural fluid","Bloody pleural fluid","Elevated serum creatine kinase","Elevated pleural triglycerides"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. In pleurodynia, the virus, often coxsackie B, targets the striated intercostal muscles, causing inflammation or necrosis that frequently raises serum creatine kinase. Elevated creatine kinase is its listed diagnostic finding, and the chest radiograph is usually normal. — Pleural Disorders 2026.pptx, Slides 98, 105, 108, 116, 118"},{"q":"When an arterial blood gas is read to find the primary disorder, which value is examined first?","choices":["The anion gap","The pH","The bicarbonate","The carbon dioxide"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The pH comes first: below 7.35 is acidemia and above 7.45 is alkalemia. Only then are the carbon dioxide and bicarbonate checked to see which one matches the pH direction, naming the disorder respiratory or metabolic. — PAJ5510 Acid Base Disorders II MBF2026sc.pptx, Slides 56, 58, 59, 63"},{"q":"A 74-year-old woman is drowsy 2 days after hip surgery. She takes an opioid for pain, a benzodiazepine at night for anxiety, a thiazide for hypertension and an inhaled bronchodilator for asthma. Arterial blood gas: pH 7.26 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 62 mm Hg (normal 35 to 45 mm Hg), bicarbonate 27 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L). Which of her medicines should be stopped?","choices":["The inhaled bronchodilator alone","The opioid and the benzodiazepine","The thiazide diuretic alone","The thiazide and the bronchodilator"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Low pH with a high carbon dioxide and a bicarbonate only slightly raised is an acute respiratory acidosis. Treatment starts with the cause and a medicine review: stop respiratory depressants, narcotics and benzodiazepines, which reduce ventilation. — PAJ5510 Acid Base Disorders II MBF2026sc.pptx, Slides 32, 36"},{"q":"What is the main downside of annual lung cancer screening?","choices":["Missed early cancers","Delayed treatment","False positives","High radiation dose"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Screening has a high incidence of false positives that prove benign, so patients receive an unnecessary workup that could have complications, such as those of a biopsy. The upside is early detection, which allows rapid intervention and an improved prognosis. — Pulmonary Nodules and Neoplasms 2026.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"A 70-year-old woman with a 50 pack-year history has a new, solid 25-mm nodule in the right upper lobe with a spiculated (spiky) border. She has no symptoms. Her clinician judges the probability of malignancy to be high. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Surveillance computed tomography","Standard staging evaluation","Annual low-dose screening","Repeat chest radiograph"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. For a new solid nodule of 8 to 30 mm with a high probability of malignancy, the next step is standard stage evaluation, with or without fludeoxyglucose positron emission tomography. If no metastases are identified, video-assisted thoracoscopic surgery with resection follows. Her upper-lobe location, spiky border and heavy smoking support the high estimate. — Pulmonary Nodules and Neoplasms 2026.pptx, Slides 11, 14, 16"},{"q":"A 61-year-old man with a 40 pack-year history was diagnosed with peripheral lung adenocarcinoma after months of cough and chest tightness. He asks which new symptoms would suggest the cancer has advanced. Which findings would signal later disease?","choices":["Cough and chest tightness","Small bowel metastases","Hemoptysis and fatigue","Bone pain and weight loss"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Adenocarcinoma is mostly asymptomatic early and usually presents first with respiratory symptoms: cough, shortness of breath, chest tightness or pain, hemoptysis and fatigue. Later signs are bone pain, weight loss and other systemic, paraneoplastic issues, and two thirds of patients present only once the disease is advanced or metastatic. — Pulmonary Nodules and Neoplasms 2026.pptx, Slides 31, 33"},{"q":"A 29-year-old woman with pleurisy has sharp pain at the base of the right chest and also pain at the tip of her right shoulder, although her shoulder examination is normal. She asks why her shoulder hurts. Which explanation is accurate?","choices":["Intercostal nerve irritation","Visceral pleura pain fibers","Phrenic nerve irritation","A strained shoulder muscle"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The parietal pleura has sensory innervation from intercostal and phrenic nerve fibers. When the inflamed parietal pleura lies on the central diaphragm, which is innervated by phrenic fibers, pain may be felt in the shoulder or neck on the same side, as hers is. — Pleural Disorders 2026.pptx, Slides 3, 9, 11, 25"},{"q":"A 79-year-old man with untreated obstructive sleep apnea has daytime drowsiness and nocturia. His daughter asks whether treatment is worthwhile at his age. What is accurate?","choices":["Nocturia is unrelated to his apnea","Treatment can improve both symptoms","His apnea mortality exceeds a younger man's","His age makes treatment pointless"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Mortality from obstructive sleep apnea is lower in the elderly because they compensate, but they still have drowsiness and nocturia, and both improve with continuous positive airway pressure. Treating him targets the symptoms that affect his daily life. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 22, 7"},{"q":"What defines massive (high-risk) pulmonary embolism?","choices":["Pleuritic pain with hemoptysis","Sustained hypotension","Normotension with high troponin","A Wells score above 4"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Massive, high-risk embolism is defined by sustained hypotension (systolic blood pressure below 90 for more than 15 minutes), pulselessness or profound bradycardia, with circulatory collapse. It carries high mortality and requires immediate advanced intervention. — Pulmonary Vascular Disorders 2026 - Gray.pptx, Slides 50, 53, 58"},{"q":"A 49-year-old man with alcoholic ketoacidosis has vomited repeatedly for 2 days. Arterial blood gas: pH 7.36 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 33 mm Hg (normal 35 to 45 mm Hg), bicarbonate 18 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L); sodium 136 mEq/L (normal 135 to 145 mEq/L), chloride 90 mEq/L (normal 98 to 106 mEq/L). Using 10 as the normal anion gap and 25 mEq/L as the normal bicarbonate, what does comparing the change in anion gap with the change in bicarbonate show?","choices":["An added metabolic alkalosis","An added respiratory alkalosis","An added normal-gap acidosis","A raised-gap acidosis alone"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Gap = 136 - (90 + 18) = 28, change in gap = 18; change in bicarbonate = 25 - 18 = 7. The gap rose far more than the bicarbonate fell, so a metabolic alkalosis from vomiting is holding bicarbonate up, detectable here because the gap is high. — PAJ5510 Acid Base Disorders II MBF2026sc.pptx, Slides 50, 27, 45, 53"},{"q":"A 47-year-old man with cardiac sarcoidosis has corticosteroid-refractory disease. He also has rheumatoid arthritis, and methotrexate he once took for it was stopped after it was implicated in lung injury. His team is weighing methotrexate, azathioprine, infliximab and continued corticosteroids. Which of these should be avoided?","choices":["Infliximab","Azathioprine","Continued corticosteroids","Methotrexate"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Methotrexate is one of the immunosuppressants used for corticosteroid-refractory sarcoidosis, but it has also been implicated as a cause of interstitial lung disease, and in him it already was. Azathioprine or infliximab covers the same refractory indication without that history. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 12, 18, 46, 47"},{"q":"A 41-year-old woman with acute respiratory distress syndrome after aspiration remains hypoxemic despite low tidal volume ventilation, careful positive end-expiratory pressure titration and early prone positioning. Her arterial oxygen pressure is 51 mm Hg on a fraction of inspired oxygen of 0.90. Which option is reserved for her situation?","choices":["High-frequency oscillatory ventilation","Extracorporeal membrane oxygenation","Inotropes for supranormal oxygen delivery","Liberal fluid resuscitation"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Her ratio is 51 / 0.90, about 57 mm Hg, well under 100, so she has severe acute respiratory distress syndrome that is refractory to ventilation strategy and prone positioning. Extracorporeal membrane oxygenation is the salvage option reserved for severe, refractory cases. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 72, 73, 74"},{"q":"After needle decompression of a tension pneumothorax, which step follows?","choices":["Chest tube insertion","Catheter aspiration","Chemical pleurodesis","Observation with oxygen"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Needle decompression is followed by moderate or large-size chest tube insertion and admission; immediate chest tube placement would have been ideal had a tube been readily available. The needle relieves the tension, and the chest tube then drains the pleural space. — Pleural Disorders 2026.pptx, Slides 39, 53, 57, 66"},{"q":"Which is a risk factor for central sleep apnea?","choices":["Enlarged tonsils","Retrognathia","Opioid medications","Macroglossia"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Opioid medications are a risk factor for central sleep apnea, with heart failure, stroke, hypoxia, high altitude and use of continuous positive airway pressure. When opioids are the cause, the first management step is to reduce them. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 6, 24, 26"},{"q":"A 46-year-old woman feels tired and sleepy most days, and her husband has seen her stop breathing and gasp during sleep. She does not snore loudly and is not treated for high blood pressure. Her body mass index is 37 kg/m2 and her neck (shirt collar) measures 15 inches. Using the STOP-Bang (snoring, tiredness, observed apnea, pressure, body mass index, age, neck, gender) questionnaire, which result fits her?","choices":["Score 2, low risk","Score 3, intermediate risk","Score 3, high risk","Score 4, intermediate risk"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Snoring 0 + tired 1 + observed apnea 1 + pressure 0 + body mass index 37 (more than 35) 1 + age 46 0 + neck 15 inches 0 + female 0 = 3. By count that is intermediate, but yes to 2 or more of the first 4 questions plus a body mass index above 35 is a high-risk rule, so she is high risk. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 16, 17"},{"q":"A 58-year-old woman with pulmonary arterial hypertension has ankle edema. When the examiner applies sustained, moderate pressure over her liver, her jugular venous pressure rises by 3 cm and stays up. What does this hepatojugular reflux indicate?","choices":["Low right-sided filling pressure","Low systemic vascular resistance","Elevated right-sided filling pressure","Elevated left atrial filling pressure"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Hepatojugular reflux is a rise in jugular venous pressure of more than 1 cm with sustained, moderate pressure on the liver, and it correlates with elevated right-sided filling pressures. In pulmonary hypertension it marks a right ventricle that cannot take the extra venous return. — Pulmonary Vascular Disorders 2026 - Gray.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"A 72-year-old man has a biopsy-proven 3.6-cm non-small cell carcinoma in the left lower lobe. Staging shows no lymph node involvement and no distant metastasis. Which stage is his cancer?","choices":["Stage 1A","Stage 1B","Stage 2A","Stage 3A"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A tumor more than 3 cm but no more than 4 cm in its greatest dimension, with no lymph node spread and no metastasis, is stage 1B. His 3.6-cm tumor with clear nodes sits in that band; at this stage the tumor may or may not involve the main bronchus or the lung's inner lining. — Pulmonary Nodules and Neoplasms 2026.pptx, Slides 22, 23, 24, 26"},{"q":"A 23-year-old tall, thin man has acute dyspnea and sharp right-sided pleuritic chest pain that began at rest. His respiratory rate is 20 breaths per minute, heart rate 98 beats per minute, blood pressure 122/76 mm Hg and oxygen saturation 96% on room air, and he speaks in whole sentences. Which is the initial test?","choices":["Supine chest radiograph","Arterial blood gas","Upright chest radiograph","Chest computed tomography"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The initial test for pneumothorax is a standard upright chest radiograph. The diagnosis is established by showing the visceral pleural line, a thin, sharp line convex toward the chest wall with no bronchovascular markings beyond it, and his stable vital signs leave time for the film. — Pleural Disorders 2026.pptx, Slides 26, 29, 33, 34, 35"},{"q":"A 41-year-old man with a dry cough and exertional dyspnea is being evaluated for interstitial lung disease. Which chest film finding would point toward sarcoidosis or silicosis rather than idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis?","choices":["Hilar and mediastinal lymphadenopathy","Progressive fibrosis over several areas","Increased markings in the lower lung fields","Pleural plaques at the lung bases"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Hilar and mediastinal lymphadenopathy on a chest film points to sarcoidosis or silicosis. Sarcoidosis shows symmetric bilateral hilar and right paratracheal adenopathy, and silicosis can show eggshell calcification of the periphery of the hilar lymph nodes. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 26, 27, 34, 35, 44, 58, 63"},{"q":"A 72-year-old malnourished woman from a nursing home has a urinary infection, hypotension and a respiratory rate of 30. Arterial blood gas: pH 7.44 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 26 mm Hg (normal 35 to 45 mm Hg), bicarbonate 17 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L); sodium 134 mEq/L (normal 135 to 145 mEq/L), chloride 105 mEq/L (normal 98 to 106 mEq/L), albumin 1.7 g/dL (normal 3.5 to 5.5 g/dL). Which acid-base disorders are present?","choices":["Normal-gap metabolic acidosis with respiratory alkalosis","Raised-gap metabolic acidosis with appropriate compensation","Raised-gap metabolic acidosis with respiratory acidosis","Raised-gap metabolic acidosis with respiratory alkalosis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Gap = 134 - (105 + 17) = 12, which looks normal, but corrected gap = 12 + 2.5 x (4.5 - 1.7) = 12 + 7 = 19, raised. Expected carbon dioxide = 1.5 x 17 + 8 = 33.5, range 31.5 to 35.5; her 26 is below it, a concurrent respiratory alkalosis, which brings the pH back to normal in sepsis. — PAJ5510 Acid Base Disorders II MBF2026sc.pptx, Slides 8, 28, 49"},{"q":"A 64-year-old man has an ileal loop bladder (a ureteral diversion) after bladder cancer surgery, takes isoniazid for latent tuberculosis, and has type 2 diabetes treated with metformin. Serum ketones are negative. Arterial blood gas: pH 7.34 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 31 mm Hg (normal 35 to 45 mm Hg), bicarbonate 16 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L). Serum sodium 139 mEq/L (normal 135 to 145 mEq/L), chloride 113 mEq/L (normal 98 to 106 mEq/L), bicarbonate 16 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L). Which part of his history best accounts for this blood gas?","choices":["Isoniazid","Diabetic ketoacidosis, active","Metformin","The ileal loop bladder"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Low pH and bicarbonate make a metabolic acidosis, and the gap is 139 - (16 + 113) = 10, normal, with a high chloride. Ureteral diversion and the ileal loop bladder are on the normal-gap list. Isoniazid and metformin raise the gap. — PAJ5510 Acid Base Disorders II MBF2026sc.pptx, Slides 44, 46, 47"},{"q":"A 77-year-old woman takes a thiazide for hypertension and has had 3 days of weakness. Her blood pressure is 126/74 mm Hg. Arterial blood gas: pH 7.47 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 47 mm Hg (normal 35 to 45 mm Hg), bicarbonate 33 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L). Her potassium is 3.1 mEq/L (normal 3.5 to 5.0 mEq/L), and her urine chloride is low. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Add a loop diuretic to the thiazide therapy","Stop the thiazide; give fluid and potassium","Start hydrocortisone and continue the thiazide","Restrict her salt and fluid intake for now"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A pH of 7.47 with a bicarbonate of 33 and a low urine chloride is a saline-responsive metabolic alkalosis, and diuretics are a cause. Treatment stops the contributor and treats the fluid and potassium deficits, without overcorrecting. — PAJ5510 Acid Base Disorders II MBF2026sc.pptx, Slides 51, 53, 54"}]};
